When the input is high-dimensional, a grid of uniformly spaced samples along every dimension may result in a large number of initial samples, and running the original expensive physics model for all those samples may be unaffordable with limited computational resources. Therefore the initial set of training samples in the proposed approach is generated using an optimal symmetric Latin hypercube (OSLH) sampling technique. This technique helps to achieve the best coverage of the input domain for any given number of samples. The testing sample set is also generated separately using OSLH. We denote the initial training samples as \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
